.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "September 2026" "conn 0.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- every project and its processes, in one window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn restart
.br
.B conn \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A terminal UI. It lists each open project's shells, dev servers and agents, and
marks the ones waiting for input. macOS and Linux. Processes keep running when
the window is closed.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBconn\fR is a terminal UI for working on many projects at once. It lists every
git repository under your project roots, what runs in each, and the shells you
open there, and marks the rows that need you.
.TP
1-2.
conn is a tmux client. It brings up a tmux server of its own, attaches the
terminal, and runs the navigator down the left of the home window. Every shell
is a tmux pane: the focused one is drawn beside the navigator, the rest wait in
windows of their own. Close the terminal and everything keeps running; run
\fBconn\fR again and it is where it was.
.TP
1-3.
conn needs \fBtmux\fR, which holds and draws the shells, and \fBlsof\fR, which
reads the process list. macOS ships lsof; \fBbrew install tmux\fR. On Linux,
install both from the package manager. \fBdocker\fR is optional: with it, a
project's containers are listed too (Section 6).
.TP
1-4.
Install the release built for this machine:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ curl -fsSL https://conn.w0zro.com/install.sh | sh
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-5.
The script verifies the download against \fBchecksums.txt\fR, puts the binary in
\fB~/.local/bin\fR and the manpage under \fB~/.local/share/man\fR. \fBman
conn\fR is this manual.
.PP
.I "Table 1-1. Install script overrides"
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_INSTALL_DIR\fR
where the binary goes
.TP
\fBCONN_MAN_DIR\fR
where the manpage goes
.TP
\fBCONN_VERSION\fR
a release other than the latest
.RE
.TP
1-6.
From source, with Go 1.25 or later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ go install github.com/w0zro/conn@latest
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ conn
.fi
.RE
.TP
1-7.
There is nothing to set up. The list opens showing what is running, so on a
quiet machine it says \fBnothing running\fR. Press \fB.\fR to show every
repository, \fB/\fR to find one by name. conn looks under \fB~/projects\fR;
Section 9 points it elsewhere.
.SH THE NAVIGATOR
.TP
2-1.
The left column is a tree, at most four levels deep: \fBgroup\fR →
\fBrepository\fR → \fBsub\-project\fR → \fBprocess\fR. A group, a repository
and a sub-project are each a place: a row a shell can open at. A repository is
any git repository under a root. The scan skips \fBnode_modules\fR,
\fBvendor\fR, \fBtarget\fR, \fB.build\fR, \fBPods\fR, \fB.venv\fR, \fBvenv\fR,
every directory whose name begins with a dot, and whatever \fBskipDirs\fR names.
.TP
2-2.
A folder inside a root that holds two or more repositories is a group. It is a
place of its own: \fBs\fR opens a shell there, \fBr\fR runs its plan, \fBx\fR
covers everything beneath it.
.TP
2-3.
In a monorepo, a directory with a manifest is a sub-project and gets a row under
its repository. Manifests, in the order they are trusted: \fB.conn\fR,
\fBProcfile\fR, \fBpackage.json\fR, \fBdeno.json\fR, \fBcomposer.json\fR,
\fBgo.mod\fR, \fBCargo.toml\fR, \fBGemfile\fR, \fBmix.exs\fR,
\fBpyproject.toml\fR, \fBpom.xml\fR, \fBbuild.gradle\fR, \fBbuild.gradle.kts\fR.
Ignored directories are skipped; an untracked \fB.conn\fR still counts.
.TP
2-4.
Each place lists the processes running in it, attributed to the innermost place
they belong to and named by what you would call them: \fBnpm run dev\fR, not
\fBnode\fR. A row that listens carries its port, \fBnpm run dev · :5173\fR; an
agent's row carries its model; a shell a plan or a task started is named for its
entry. Dim rows have no shell of their own to go to: processes started outside
conn, and processes a shell's command started in turn, a claude's tools, say.
\fBenter\fR reaches as far as the shell above them. All are listed and killable.
The row that is conn itself says \fB(me)\fR.
.TP
2-5.
A row's mark says how its command ended, or that its process is unwell. An
agent's marks are in Section 5.
.PP
.I "Table 2-1. Row marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB✗\fR
in red: the command ended badly, or the process is stopped, in an
uninterruptible wait, or a zombie. Beside the name, what the run said of itself
and how long ago: \fBtest · 3 failed · 3m\fR; or, when it said nothing and a
signal ended it, what ended it: \fBworker · killed · 3m\fR. The pane and the
checklist decode the status the same way: 137 is killed, 143 terminated, 130
interrupted, 127 not found
.TP
\fB✓\fR
in green: the command ended well. \fBtest · 12 passed · 2h\fR
.RE
.TP
2-6.
While the navigator has focus, it fills the pane beside the list with what is
known about the row under the cursor. For a place: its branch, status, upstream
and last commit; its plan as a checklist, each entry up, down or ended and how;
its tasks and how their last run went. For a process: its run, arguments,
uptime, cpu, memory, state, ports and children, and beneath the facts the last
of what its shell has shown, in the shell's own colors. For a container: its
name, image, status and the last of its logs. What a thing is doing can be read
without entering it.
.TP
2-7.
tmux draws the status line at the foot. It begins with \fBCONN\fR in purple,
then the mode (Table 2-2), then anything conn has to say until the next key: a
report, an error, a confirmation, or that a newer release is out. What a chord
says from a shell shows there too, for a few seconds. The right corner names the
kind of agent \fBa\fR starts.
.PP
.I "Table 2-2. Modes on the status line"
.RS
.TP
\fBNAV\fR
the navigator has focus
.TP
\fBPROC\fR
a shell has focus
.TP
\fBPREFIX\fR
a chord hangs after ctrl-space
.TP
\fBCOPY\fR
tmux copy mode
.TP
\fB/query\fR
a query being typed, or the filter standing
.TP
\fBCONTINUE\fR
the picker of conversations is open
.TP
\fBNEW\fR
a project's path is being typed
.TP
\fBCONFIRM\fR
a kill waits on its second key
.RE
.TP
2-8.
tmux handles the mouse, and conn adds no bindings of its own: the wheel scrolls
a pane's transcript, a drag selects and copies on release, a click focuses the
pane under it, and a program that speaks mouse gets its own clicks and wheel.
Hold shift to select the terminal's own way. A program that copies for itself,
vim's clipboard yank or anything speaking OSC 52, reaches the system clipboard
through tmux.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A click into the navigator focuses it, as \fBctrl\-space \-\fR does: the shell
beside it returns to its own window.
.SH MOVING
.TP
3-1.
In the list:
.PP
.I "Table 3-1. Moving in the list"
.RS
.TP
\fBj k\fR
move; the arrows move too
.TP
\fBgg G\fR
top and bottom
.TP
\fBspace\fR
fold or unfold the selected node; a folded node shows +n for what it hides
.TP
\fB\-\fR
fold or unfold everything; unfolded rows also show pids, and a container's id
.TP
\fB.\fR
everything, or only what is running
.TP
\fBJ K\fR
focus the next and previous shell, in the list's order
.RE
.TP
3-2.
\fB/\fR finds. Each word of the query must appear in order inside what a row
says, not contiguously: \fBtsl\fR finds tressle-api, \fBmono api\fR finds the
api inside mono, and the letters that matched are lit. A place answers by its
name; a process by anything its row says: the command, its arguments, the plan's
name for it, an agent's name and model, a port. Processes that answer are listed
under their places, pruned to the branches that answer. An empty query lists
places alone.
.TP
3-3.
A search is a way of getting somewhere, so the keys that go somewhere work while
the query is still being typed. Acting ends the search; the filter holds, so the
subject stays listed. \fBesc\fR cancels the search and puts the cursor back
where it was. The query takes readline's editing keys: \fBctrl\-w\fR,
\fBctrl\-u\fR, \fBctrl\-h\fR, \fBctrl\-e\fR and the rest.
.PP
.I "Table 3-2. Keys while a query is typed"
.RS
.TP
\fB↑↓ ctrl\-p ctrl\-n\fR
move through what is left
.TP
\fBenter\fR
open a shell on a place; step into a found process's shell
.TP
\fBctrl\-r\fR
run the plan there
.TP
\fBctrl\-a\fR
start an agent there
.TP
\fBtab\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBshift+tab\fR
back: the previous shell, or the list
.TP
\fBctrl\-x\fR
ask to kill what the cursor is on
.TP
\fBesc\fR
cancel the search
.RE
.TP
3-4.
\fBenter\fR on a shell's row, or \fBs\fR on a place, focuses a shell beside the
navigator. From then on the shell receives every keystroke but one:
\fBctrl\-space\fR, the prefix, tmux's own under conn's configuration, which
reaches conn from any shell. \fBctrl\-space \-\fR focuses the navigator again.
\fB?\fR, in the navigator or after the prefix, lists every key in a popup over
the window; Appendix A lists them here. Every key tmux would otherwise bind is
unbound.
.TP
3-5.
\fBtab\fR at the list, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR anywhere, goes to the next
thing that needs you: an agent waiting, a command that ended badly, a process
stopped or a zombie. Again continues around them. A held shell is entered, at
its prompt or transcript; a process conn only watches gets the cursor.
.TP
3-6.
\fBshift+tab\fR at the list, or \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR anywhere, returns
focus to where it was: the shell before this one, whether or not focus passed
through the navigator on the way, or the navigator when focus came from it.
Again returns. Before focus has moved at all, it is the list from a shell and
the cursor's shell from the list.
.SH SHELLS
.TP
4-1.
\fBs\fR opens \fB$SHELL\fR at the selected place, beside the navigator, focused.
A shell you enter moves in beside the navigator; when the navigator takes focus
again it moves out to a tmux window of its own, where it keeps running, and the
navigator has the whole window. Each shell keeps 10,000 lines of transcript;
\fBscrollback\fR in the configuration raises that.
.TP
4-2.
\fBn\fR makes a new project. The status line asks where, on a line prefilled
with the cursor's folder relative to its root, \fBw0zro/\fR for a cursor on a
repository in that folder, and the name goes on the end. Backspace the folder
away for the root itself; type another and it is made. A path from \fB~\fR or
\fB/\fR is taken as it is. \fBenter\fR makes the directory, runs \fBgit init\fR
in it and opens a shell there; the cursor lands on the project once the scan
lists it. A directory already there is left alone. \fBesc\fR cancels.
.TP
4-3.
\fBq\fR in the navigator, or \fBctrl\-space q\fR anywhere, detaches the
terminal. The shells keep running. Quit the terminal, come back, run \fBconn\fR:
everything is where it was.
.SH AGENTS
.TP
5-1.
\fBa\fR starts an agent at the selected place, in a shell like any other: Claude
Code, unless the configuration's \fBagent\fR says otherwise. Claude Code reports
its state, and the navigator marks each instance:
.PP
.I "Table 5-1. Agent marks"
.RS
.TP
\fB⠇\fR
working
.TP
\fB◆\fR
blocked mid-turn: a permission prompt, a question
.TP
\fB●\fR
finished its turn; waiting for the next ask
.TP
\fB○\fR
idle since it started
.RE
.TP
5-2.
The ◆ is the one to answer first: answering it resumes work already underway.
The pane for a claude row names what it is waiting on, the last thing asked of
it, and what it says it is doing. \fBtab\fR, or \fBctrl\-space enter\fR from any
shell, goes to it (3-5).
.TP
5-3.
A conversation does not end when its instance exits; it is suspended, and the
transcript is enough to pick it back up. \fBA\fR on a place lists what is
suspended there, sub-projects included and, for a group, its repositories,
newest first: when each last moved, its branch, and the last thing asked of it.
Typing narrows the list, \fBenter\fR continues the one under the cursor in a
shell opened where it was had, \fBesc\fR closes the list. A conversation an
instance still carries is not offered. Every kind that keeps conversations
answers the picker, and each is continued by the kind that had it.
.TP
5-4.
conn knows agents as kinds: \fBclaude\fR and \fBollama\fR. \fB,\fR at the
navigator, or \fBctrl\-space ,\fR from any shell, moves \fBa\fR on to the next
kind, around the end, for the whole server until it goes; the status line's
right corner names it. Ollama's rows are plain processes, unmarked. It runs the
model most recently pulled or run, read from the manifests under
\fB~/.ollama/models\fR or \fB$OLLAMA_MODELS\fR, so the pick needs no daemon; the
REPL does, and says so when it is down. With nothing pulled, \fBa\fR runs
\fBollama list\fR instead. \fBagentRuns\fR in the configuration names what a
kind runs when its own answer is not the wanted one.
.SH PLANS
.TP
6-1.
Working on a project usually means the same processes every time. A project
lists them in \fB.conn\fR, Procfile format, one name and command per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dev:   npm run dev
api:   go run ./cmd/api
agent: claude
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-1. A plan"
.TP
6-2.
Without one, conn reads the project's own files, in this order:
.PP
.I "Table 6-1. Where a plan comes from"
.RS
.TP
\fB.conn\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBProcfile\fR
every line is an entry
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
one entry: its \fBdev\fR script, else its \fBstart\fR script
.TP
\fBcompose.yaml\fR
one entry, \fBcompose: docker compose up\fR, since compose runs every service
and attaches to each; \fBcompose.yml\fR, \fBdocker\-compose.yml\fR and
\fBdocker\-compose.yaml\fR the same
.RE
.TP
6-3.
\fBr\fR starts the entries that are not running, each in a window named for it,
and puts the cursor on the first. It is a list to run, not a supervisor: nothing
restarts on its own. When an entry's command ends, its shell stays at the prompt
with the transcript, the row shows ✗ or ✓, the pane's checklist says how and
when it ended, and \fBr\fR starts it again beside the old shell. Running
anything by hand in that shell clears the mark: the exit has been seen.
.TP
6-4.
A project on docker runs its services in containers, which the process scan
cannot see. compose writes on every container the directory it was started for,
the same fact lsof reports of a process, so a container is filed under its place
by the rule everything else is: a row named for its service, with the ports it
publishes on the host, \fBweb · :8438\fR. It sits under the \fBdocker compose
up\fR that runs it when one runs in a shell, and under the place itself when
compose was left detached. \fBenter\fR on it opens a shell inside it —
compose's \fBexec\fR, in the place — held like any shell; its pane says the
container's name, image and status, with the last of its logs. A service that
died beside the others stays listed while the project runs, showing the cross
and how long ago — \fBworker · 3m ✗\fR — and \fBtab\fR goes to it; one
that exited well shows the check. A health check's word stands where the ports
would while it says anything but healthy: \fBapi · unhealthy ✗\fR, or
starting. What the machine runs for you outside every project is listed under
\fBglobal\fR, at the foot of the list, there while anything is in it: containers
of no project — a \fBdocker run\fR, or compose from elsewhere — and
processes listening on a port they chose from no project’s directory, an
\fBollama serve\fR, a brew postgres. The desktop’s own listeners stay out, and
docker’s proxy, which the containers already say. \fBx\fR, \fBenter\fR and the
pane work as under a project; the place is nowhere on disk, so no shell opens in
it and no project is made there.
.PP
.RS
.nf
compose-demo
   app
     cache · :6390
     web · :8438
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 6-2. A compose project's containers, under the compose that runs them"
.TP
6-5.
What \fBx\fR stopped, \fBr\fR brings back where it was. The services the place's
compose file declares are what it needs: \fBr\fR starts the ones that are down
with \fBdocker compose up \-d\fR for them, which starts a stopped container and
makes again one that was removed, and a compose up running in a shell there
takes the service's logs up again, so the shape holds. An entry \fBr\fR is
starting that runs compose itself brings everything up instead. A service that a
plan entry is named for is left to the entry.
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A plan is not a supervisor. An ended entry's shell stays at its prompt, with its
transcript, until \fBx\fR closes it or \fBr\fR starts the entry again beside it.
.SH TASKS
.TP
7-1.
\fBt\fR, \fBb\fR and \fBl\fR run the place's tests, build and lint as the
project itself defines them. What the project wrote down comes first: a Makefile
target, a justfile recipe, a Taskfile task or a package.json script named
\fBtest\fR, \fBbuild\fR or \fBlint\fR. Otherwise the ecosystem's own command, by
the file that marks the project. Only a linter the ecosystem ships with is
guessed at; a project on ruff or eslint names it in a script. A project the
guess is wrong for says so in its Makefile, which conn checks first.
.PP
.I "Table 7-1. How a task's command is found"
.RS
.TP
\fBMakefile, justfile, Taskfile\fR
the target, recipe or task named for the task
.TP
\fBpackage.json\fR
the script named for the task, run with npm
.TP
\fBgo.mod\fR
\fBgo test ./...\fR, \fBgo build ./...\fR, \fBgo vet ./...\fR
.TP
\fBCargo.toml\fR
\fBcargo test\fR, \fBcargo build\fR, \fBcargo clippy\fR
.TP
\fBpyproject.toml\fR
\fBpytest\fR; \fBpython3 manage.py test\fR for a Django project
.TP
\fBGemfile\fR
\fBrspec\fR with a spec directory, else \fBrake test\fR
.TP
\fBmix.exs and the rest\fR
the ecosystem's own: mix, gradle, mvn, deno, swift, dune
.RE
.TP
7-2.
Each run is a shell named for its task, so its row shows ✓ or ✗, \fBtab\fR
goes to a failure, and the place's pane says how each task's last run went and
how long ago. The key again replaces a finished run rather than stacking another
beside it; a run still going is left to finish. \fBconn test\fR, \fBconn
build\fR, \fBconn lint\fR and \fBctrl\-space t\fR, \fBb\fR, \fBl\fR do the same
from any shell.
.TP
7-3.
conn reads the end of the transcript by the shape of the tool's summary line: go
test, pytest, jest, vitest, mocha, cargo, rspec, ExUnit, golangci-lint, tsc,
ruff, eslint. A failed run's row says \fBtest · 3 failed\fR, a passed one \fB12
passed\fR. A shape it does not know leaves the status alone.
.SH STOPPING
.I "Table 8-1. Kill keys"
.RS
.TP
\fBx\fR
kill the selected process
.TP
\fBX\fR
kill it, everything under it, and what shares its process group
.TP
\fBx on an entry\fR
end the entry's command and its shell; \fBr\fR starts it again
.TP
\fBx on a container\fR
stop the container, docker's way
.TP
\fBx on a place\fR
kill everything running in it
.TP
\fBx on a signalled row\fR
kill outright: SIGKILL to what has not gone
.TP
\fBR\fR
end the server and every shell it holds
.RE
.TP
8-1.
Every kill asks first: \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR or \fBenter\fR confirms, anything else
cancels. Processes get SIGTERM. The question offers the other signals: \fB9\fR
confirms with SIGKILL, \fBi\fR with SIGINT, for a server that only tears down on
ctrl-c, and \fBh\fR with SIGHUP, for a daemon that reloads on it. A signalled
row shows a red marker until a scan observes the exit; the list never claims an
exit it has not seen. A process that has not gone in five seconds is given up
on, and the status line says so; \fBx\fR on it then, or on a row still on its
way out, asks to kill it outright, with SIGKILL, which nothing refuses. Kills
work on any listed process, including ones conn did not start.
.TP
8-2.
A held shell is closed through the server. One still running its command is
closed only once the command has gone: the command gets SIGTERM, and the shell
is hung up when a scan finds it back at its prompt, so a \fBdocker compose up\fR
stops its containers on the way out; hung up first, it would leave them running.
An entry's row is the entry, and the question names it: \fBkill app?\fR The
entry is then gone, as an entry nothing runs in is, and \fBr\fR starts it again.
An exit that \fBx\fR asked for shows no mark. A tree kill, or an entry's, covers
the process group as well as the tree: a parent that exits leaves its children
to init and the tree loses them, but the job keeps them. Such a process is
listed under its job's leader while the leader runs, and the pane says
\fBorphaned\fR of it.
.TP
8-3.
A container is stopped by docker, which signals the process inside it and
follows with SIGKILL after its grace. \fBr\fR brings the service back (6-5).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
9-1.
One JSON file, entirely optional, at \fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR;
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is honored.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  \(dqprojectsDirs\(dq: [\(dq~/projects\(dq, \(dq~/work/mono\(dq],
  \(dqscrollback\(dq: 50000,
  \(dqnavWidth\(dq: 34,
  \(dqskipDirs\(dq: [\(dqbazel-out\(dq, \(dqdist\(dq]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "Figure 9-1. A configuration"
.TP
9-2.
Every field has a default; a file that names none is the same as no file.
.PP
.I "Table 9-1. Configuration fields"
.RS
.TP
\fBprojectsDir\fR
the directory searched for repositories; ~ and $VARS expand (default
$HOME/projects)
.TP
\fBprojectsDirs\fR
several roots; when set, projectsDir is ignored. A root that only exists on
another machine is fine
.TP
\fBscrollback\fR
transcript lines each shell keeps. Written into the server's configuration at
launch, so raising it takes a fresh server (10-3) (default 10000)
.TP
\fBnavWidth\fR
the navigator column's width, 16–60 (default 30)
.TP
\fBtheme\fR
the side tmux draws for, dark or light; the navigator uses the terminal's own
sixteen colors (default dark)
.TP
\fBagent\fR
the kind of agent \fBa\fR starts, until \fB,\fR says otherwise: claude or ollama
(default claude)
.TP
\fBagentRuns\fR
what starting a kind runs, by name: {\(dqollama\(dq: \(dqollama run mistral\(dq}
.TP
\fBskipDirs\fR
directory names the scan never enters, on top of the built-in list
.RE
.SH THE SERVER
.TP
10-1.
The shells live in a tmux server of conn's own: one per user, for all projects,
on a socket at \fB~/.local/state/conn/tmux\-<uid>.sock\fR. \fBCONN_SOCKET\fR
moves the socket; \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR moves the directory. Every \fBconn\fR
writes the server's configuration, the prefix and its chords, the status line,
the transcript cap, to \fB~/.local/state/conn/tmux.conf\fR, brings the server up
under it or sources it into one already running, and attaches. No .tmux.conf
reaches it.
.TP
10-2.
The session's first window is the home window: the navigator in the pane down
its left, at \fBnavWidth\fR through every resize, and the focused shell beside
it. Close the navigator and \fBctrl\-space \-\fR makes it again. A \fBconn\fR
from a newer build restarts the navigator in its pane and re-reads the
configuration; the server keeps the shells. Run from a shell inside the window,
it does only that. Inside any other tmux it stops with a message, since
attaching would nest one tmux in another.
.TP
10-3.
The server lives exactly as long as it holds something: the home window brings
it up, the last window closing takes it down. \fBR\fR, or \fBctrl\-space R\fR
from any shell, ends it and every shell it holds, after asking; it is the way to
a fresh server. \fBconn restart\fR, typed outside the window, is \fBR\fR and
then \fBconn\fR as one command; inside the window it stops with a message, since
the pane running it would go with the server. \fBtmux \-S
~/.local/state/conn/tmux\-<uid>.sock attach\fR is what \fBconn\fR does.
.TP
10-4.
The chords run \fBconn\fR itself with a word, each a short command against the
server. None of them are for typing, but all of them can be:
.PP
.I "Table 10-1. Commands against the server"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn home [key]\fR
to the navigator, pressing key there
.TP
\fBconn shell [dir]\fR
a shell in dir, beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn agent [dir]\fR
an agent in dir, beside the navigator
.TP
\fBconn kind\fR
the next kind of agent, for \fBa\fR and the agent chord
.TP
\fBconn run [dir]\fR
the plan of the place holding dir
.TP
\fBconn test [dir]\fR
its tests; \fBbuild\fR and \fBlint\fR the same way
.TP
\fBconn jump\fR
the next thing that needs you
.TP
\fBconn back\fR
back: the previous shell, or the navigator
.TP
\fBconn next, prev\fR
the next and previous shell
.TP
\fBconn keys [client]\fR
the key list, in a popup over the client; \fBconn page\fR is the page inside it
.TP
\fBconn ls\fR
the held shells: pid, directory, name, exit; one per line
.TP
\fBconn restart\fR
the server ended, after asking, and brought up again under this build
.RE
.TP
10-5.
conn keeps itself current. The navigator asks github which release is latest, on
its first paint and once a day after, a plain request for the release page's
redirect, kept in \fB~/.local/state/conn/latest\-release\fR. A release newer
than the build is said on the status line whenever nothing else is: \fBconn
0.5.0 is out · U installs it\fR. \fBU\fR runs the installer in a popup, aimed
at the directory this build runs from, and then \fBconn\fR, which gives the
server the new configuration and brings the navigator back as the new build; the
shells stay. With nothing newer known, \fBU\fR asks now and says what it finds.
A build that is no release, a tree past a tag or a plain go build, is never
behind one. Upgrading by hand is the same: install the binary, and the next
\fBconn\fR does the rest.
.TP
10-6.
\fBconn ls\fR is the server's state for scripts: the held shells, one per line,
pid, directory, name and exit, tab-separated. The name is empty for a shell
opened by hand; the exit is how the shell's command ended, empty while it runs.
No server listening is an empty list, not an error.
